```plaintext
1result.sql
```

Reason:  
Both scripts try to compute the same metric and return the same example rows, but `1result.sql` follows the requested definition more rigorously:

• It explicitly removes records whose `end_station_id` is NULL when building the denominator, so every “total trips ending at that station” count is accurate.  
• It constructs the numerator with simple, transparent CTEs (loop → qualifying 2‑minute windows → group trips), guaranteeing that every trip counted really satisfies “start = end AND shared 2‑minute window with ≥2 riders.”  
• `2result.sql` leaves the NULL `end_station_id` rows in its denominator (they are grouped into a NULL station), which can slightly distort later joins, and it relies on referencing a SELECT‑alias in the same GROUP BY—something BigQuery accepts but is less portable.

Because `1result.sql` is both clearer and strictly aligned with the problem statement, it is the safer and therefore “correct” choice.